Related: #71058

## What Problem This Solves

Resolves a problem where Microsoft Teams could only be configured as one bot identity per OpenClaw gateway. That made it difficult to run multiple Teams-visible agents, each with its own Teams app/bot registration, from the same OpenClaw instance in the same way operators can use account-specific routing with other channel integrations.

## Why This Change Was Made

This adds first-class account support to the Microsoft Teams channel while preserving the legacy single-bot configuration path. `channels.msteams.accounts.<id>` now defines Teams bot accounts, `channels.msteams.defaultAccount` selects the implicit account when one is not provided, and existing root-level `appId`/`appPassword`/`tenantId`/`webhook` config continues to work as the default account.

The implementation keeps account identity fields explicit per named account: named accounts must provide their own `appId`, credential, and webhook port, while shared settings such as `tenantId`, webhook path, policies, Graph/SSO/delegated-auth settings, streaming behavior, and delivery settings can be inherited from the root and overridden per account. Inbound handling, proactive sends, replies, Graph-backed actions, SSO/delegated token storage, polls, sent-message cache, conversation state, probes, setup flows, and routing all carry the account context.

## User Impact

Users can now configure multiple Microsoft Teams bot accounts in one OpenClaw gateway and bind each account to the agent that should answer:

```json5
{
  agents: {
    list: [
      { id: "main", workspace: "~/.openclaw/workspace-main" },
      { id: "support", workspace: "~/.openclaw/workspace-support" },
    ],
  },
  bindings: [
    { agentId: "main", match: { channel: "msteams", accountId: "default" } },
    { agentId: "support", match: { channel: "msteams", accountId: "support" } },
  ],
  channels: {
    msteams: {
      enabled: true,
      tenantId: "<TENANT_ID>",
      webhook: { path: "/api/messages" },
      defaultAccount: "default",
      accounts: {
        default: {
          appId: "<PRIMARY_CLIENT_ID>",
          appPassword: "<PRIMARY_CLIENT_SECRET>",
          webhook: { port: 3978 },
        },
        support: {
          appId: "<SUPPORT_CLIENT_ID>",
          appPassword: "<SUPPORT_CLIENT_SECRET>",
          webhook: { port: 3979 },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Existing single-bot configs remain valid and do not require bindings:

```json5
{
  channels: {
    msteams: {
      enabled: true,
      appId: "<CLIENT_ID>",
      appPassword: "<CLIENT_SECRET>",
      tenantId: "<TENANT_ID>",
      webhook: { port: 3978, path: "/api/messages" },
    },
  },
}
```

## Evidence

- Local multi-account live smoke: two separate Microsoft Teams bot apps were installed in a Teams group chat and both replied through the same OpenClaw gateway using separate Teams account identities and routes. Redacted screenshot available: <img width="1432" height="2580" alt="teams_screenshot_dale_legal_blurred" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ee8e035d-9b8f-427c-9f19-3c00b3406b76" />
- Backward compatibility verified by tests covering legacy root-level single-bot config and default-account behavior.
- Account scoping verified across inbound messages, outbound/proactive replies, Graph actions, SSO/delegated token storage, polls, sent-message cache, conversation state, setup/probe flows, and routing.
- `pnpm test:extension msteams`
- `pnpm test:contracts:channels` (45 files, 305 tests)
- `pnpm build`
- `pnpm check`
- `git diff --check`
- `codex review --base upstream/main`: no actionable correctness issues found.

AI-assisted: yes. I used Codex to help implement, audit, test, and review this change, and I reviewed the behavior and code paths before opening the PR.

If this gets merged, then I will focus on enhancements next.

Related: #102175

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where long-lived embedded sessions could lose prompt-cache continuity when ordinary turns, ambient group `room_event` turns, queued work, compaction, and native Responses continuation shared one session.

The failure had three connected causes:

- turn-specific reply delivery leaked into cache-visible prompt and tool construction;
- owner/sender checks changed the prompt-visible tool catalogue from 47 schemas to 44 on some turns;
- queue, memory, compaction, and provider-reconstruction paths independently rebuilt state, while OpenClaw's built-in authenticated Responses stream could miss native delta continuation.

Strict room delivery and sender authorization are still required. The bug was making those current-turn decisions mutate reusable prompt prefixes and tool schemas.

## Why This Change Was Made

This PR separates stable model context from current-turn behavior:

- Actual source-reply delivery remains independent from the session-stable prompt-facing mode. Executable `message` tools receive the real turn mode, including the strict room-event source-reply sink.
- The model sees one stable session/runtime tool catalogue. `cron`, `gateway`, `nodes`, and `computer` are no longer removed merely because sender or event policy changes.
- One trusted `ToolAccessPolicy` resolver derives a deterministic full policy snapshot and drives the hard runtime gate. Unavailable protected tools return `TOOL_ACCESS_DENIED`, marked non-retryable, before hooks or implementations can run.
- Sender trust is explicit: owners and runtime-owned `cron`, `heartbeat`, and `memory` turns are distinct from non-owner and genuinely unknown senders. Explicit non-owner identity and ambient `room_event` policy override an internal trigger.
- Full policy snapshots are inserted before the current user content when permissions or provider route change, or when queueing, memory work, compaction, reconstruction, recovery, or fallback makes prior model state uncertain. Unchanged warm continuations do not repeat the full snapshot.
- Pi/OpenClaw persists a hidden runtime-generated current-turn carrier; Codex uses turn-scoped developer instructions; Copilot prefixes only the current turn. Reusable thread/system instructions stay stable.
- Preflight, manual, queued, and direct compaction; queue draining; memory runs; Codex; Copilot; and fallback/recovery paths all carry the same effective policy.
- The existing native GPT-5.5/GPT-5.6 Responses continuation fix remains narrow: built-in authenticated streams can use `previous_response_id` plus delta input, while custom streams keep their existing routing.

## User Impact

Users should get materially better warm-turn cache reuse without weakening room-event delivery or sender authorization.

Owner-authorized direct turns can still execute protected tools. Ambient room events and unauthorized/unknown senders still cannot execute those tools, even though their schemas remain visible for cache stability. A user message cannot grant access because authorization is checked again immediately before execution.

Queueing, memory maintenance, compaction, and runtime fallback no longer silently discard the active policy or rebuild from the wrong delivery mode.

## Evidence

Sanitized same-session traces from the original Discord reproduction showed:

- prompt-visible inventory flipping between 44 and 47 tools;
- matching system-prompt hash changes;
- cached prefixes collapsing to roughly 2k-10k tokens or ceilinging around 37k-40k;
- after stabilizing prompt/tool shape, warm turns reaching roughly 49k-52k cached tokens on approximately 51k-55k context.

The latest head, `be9ef558a98035f3d2f85f575d763dc09ba53276`, adds the turn-scoped policy and hard runtime boundary while preserving that stable schema shape. Tests prove:

- owner direct, owner room-event, non-owner room-event, and return-to-owner turns serialize identical ordered tool names, descriptions, and JSON schemas;
- policy versions change independently of schemas when sender/event access changes;
- all four protected tools remain described but denied calls cannot reach hooks or implementations, including repeated and spoof-like calls;
- a permitted owner turn still reaches the original protected-tool implementation;
- the full snapshot precedes current user content, is not repeated on unchanged continuation, and is restored after reconstruction, queue rebuilds, memory runs, fallback, recovery, and every compaction entry point;
- Codex policy changes are current-turn developer instructions, never reusable thread instructions;
- Copilot policy changes are current-turn prompt content, never reusable system instructions;
- targetless strict room-event sends still reach the internal source-reply sink, and private final text does not become visible;
- GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 second turns use `previous_response_id` with delta-only input; a changed policy is part of that delta; custom streams are not reclassified.

Validation on the exact head:

- changed regression set: 34 files, 1,231 tests passed;
- final focused policy/schema/trust check: 5 files, 169 tests passed;
- `pnpm tsgo:prod` passed;
- `pnpm check:test-types` passed;
- `pnpm tsgo:test:packages` passed;
- `pnpm lint:core` and `pnpm lint:extensions` passed;
- scoped `oxfmt --check` and `git diff --check` passed;
- extension package boundary compile passed for 120 plugins and 2 canaries;
- `pnpm build` passed;
- exact head installed locally: LaunchAgent loaded, gateway running, RPC probe OK, port 18789 listening, dashboard HTTP 200.

## Compatibility And Security Boundaries

- This does not expose every installed plugin. It stabilizes the session/runtime superset that existing construction policy already makes available.
- Prompt text is advisory. The outer runtime wrapper is the authorization boundary and preserves existing tool-specific checks on allowed calls.
- Pi's generic LLM message contract has no developer role, so its trusted policy carrier is a hidden runtime-generated current-turn custom message that projects to model input; it is not a fake tool result. Runtime authorization does not trust that message.
- No sender IDs, channel IDs, credentials, hosts, or private trace payloads appear in policy snapshots or evidence.
- No persistent data migration is required.
- The branch is intentionally not rebased on today's fast-moving `main`; maintainers can refresh it during merge preparation.

Disclaimer: This PR was filed and updated autonomously by Codex on behalf of the reporter. Private user, channel, host, credential, and full trace identifiers were removed from the evidence.

## Summary

- add dynamic configured-model choices to the native `/model` command
- enable the existing command argument menu path for `/model` so Slack can render model picks with its existing buttons/static_select/external_select machinery
- keep bare `/model` status behavior when no configured model choices are available

## Details

`/think` already gets rich Slack command UI because its registry entry declares choices plus an argument menu. `/model` had a positional argument but no choices/menu metadata, so Slack had nothing to render before dispatching the command as plain text.

This change keeps the fix in the shared command registry: configured catalog entries become `provider/model` choices, and the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter handles the selected value. No Slack-specific command renderer changes are needed.

## Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/commands-registry.test.ts`
- `git diff --check HEAD~1..HEAD`
- `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` registry smoke shown below

## Real behavior proof

Behavior addressed: Slack native slash-command UX for `/model` can now surface the existing command argument menu instead of requiring users to type a model id manually.

Real environment tested: local OpenClaw source checkout in an isolated worktree from `origin/main`, running the actual command registry module after this patch.

Exact steps or command run after this patch: ran this local terminal command against the patched checkout:

```bash
node --import tsx - <<'EOF'
import { findCommandByNativeName, resolveCommandArgMenu } from './src/auto-reply/commands-registry.ts';
const command = findCommandByNativeName('model');
if (!command) throw new Error('missing /model command');
const menu = resolveCommandArgMenu({
  command,
  cfg: {
    models: {
      providers: {
        openai: { models: [{ id: 'gpt-5.5', name: 'GPT-5.5' }] },
        anthropic: { models: [{ id: 'claude-sonnet-4.6', name: 'Claude Sonnet 4.6' }] },
      },
    },
  },
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ arg: menu?.arg.name, choices: menu?.choices }, null, 2));
EOF
```

Evidence after fix: terminal output from the patched OpenClaw registry smoke:

```json
{
  "arg": "model",
  "choices": [
    {
      "value": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
      "label": "anthropic/Claude Sonnet 4.6"
    },
    {
      "value": "openai/gpt-5.5",
      "label": "openai/GPT-5.5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Observed result after fix: `/model` now resolves a native command argument menu for configured model choices, using `provider/model` values that the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter dispatch path already accepts. Focused registry tests also passed 31/31.

What was not tested: live Slack Block Kit interaction against a real Slack workspace. The Slack plugin path reused here is already covered by existing arg-menu render/dispatch tests; this PR adds the missing registry metadata that feeds that path.

## Notes

AI-assisted with human-requested agent workflow. The local commit helper hydrated ignored `node_modules` in the isolated worktree before committing; no tracked dependency or lockfile changes are included.

